h(cell);\n} else if (!isAlive && neighbors === 3) {\n  result.push(cell);\n}\n```\n\nComponent count:\n- Constants: 2, 3, 3 → 3\n- Bindings: x, y, cell, neighbors, isAlive → 5\n- Invocations: countLiveNeighbors(), liveSet.has(), result.push() (x2) → 4 invocations × 2 = 8\n- Conditionals: outer if, `||` inside first branch, else-if, implicit `&&`s (4 boolean conditions total: `isAlive`, `neighbors===2||neighbors===3`, `!isAlive`, `neighbors===3`) → counted as 2 conditional statements (if/else-if) × 4 = 8\n- Loops: 0 (handled by outer for-of, unchanged)\n- Assignments: 0 (const declarations counted as bindings, not assignments)\n\n**Total Mass ≈ 24** (3 + 5 + 8 + 8)\n\n### Simple Design Rules Evaluation\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: All 10 tests green beforehand — confirmed baseline.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: The two-branch if/else-if obscures the actual rule: \"a cell is alive next generation exactly when it has 3 neighbors, or it's currently alive with 2 neighbors.\" This is a single boolean condition, not two separate branches with duplicated `result.push(cell)`.\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: `result.push(cell)` was duplicated in both branches — the *conclusion* was the same, only the *condition* differed. Classic case for consolidating into one boolean expression.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: An if/else-if with a duplicated push statement is more elements than needed for one boolean check.\n\n### Refactoring Applied\n\nExtracted the survival/birth conditions into a single explaining boolean, `willBeAlive`, replacing the if/else-if duplication with one `if`:\n\n```js\nconst [x, y] = cell;\nconst neighbors = countLiveNeighbors(x, y, liveSet);\nconst isAlive = liveSet.has(key);\nconst willBeAlive = neighbors === 3 || (isAlive && neighbors === 2);\n\nif (willBeAlive) {\n  result.push(cell);\n}\n```\n\n### Refactored APP Mass\n\nComponent count:\n- Constants: 3, 3, 2 → 3\n- Bindings: x, y, cell, neighbors, isAlive, willBeAlive → 6\n- Invocations: countLiveN